Abstract.Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have shown promising results in multi-task robotic manipulation by leveraging visual and language understanding for action generation. However, existing lightweight VLA models employ a single dense action expert to handle all tasks, which limits their multi-task capability under constrained computational budgets. To address this issue, we propose SmolVLA-MoE, a novel framework that introduces sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) into the FFN layers of the action expert in VLA models. SmolVLA-MoE replaces dense FFN layers with multiple parallel expert FFNs and a token-level router, enabling each token to adaptively select the most suitable expert for action generation. We further introduce a shared expert with a tunable weight parameter to balance the inheritance of pre-trained capabilities and task-specific expert specialization. Through sparse activation, SmolVLA-MoE activates only a single expert FFN (∼100M activated parameters in the action expert module) during inference, with a total activated parameter count of∼500M including the frozen VLM backbone—the same as the dense baseline SmolVLA—while benefiting from the multi-expert representational capacity provided by∼800M total stored parameters. We evaluate our method on four task suites of the LIBERO benchmark and compare it against SmolVLA and other representative baselines. Experimental results demonstrate that SmolVLAMoE achieves an average success rate of 84.25% across all four suites, outperforming the dense baseline SmolVLA (82.75%) and the much larger OpenVLA-7B (76.5%), while maintaining comparable inference efficiency, providing an effective and efficient solution for multi-task robotic manipulation. Our code is publicly available athttps://github.com/dudu435/ smolvla-moe.
