1 Introduction
Robotic manipulation in unstructured environments requires models that can understand diverse visual scenes, comprehend natural language instructions, and generate corresponding actions. Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models [3,4,22,14] have emerged as a promising paradigm to address this challenge by unifying vision, language, and action within a single framework. Recent works such as RT1 [4], RT-2 [3], Octo [22], and OpenVLA [14] have demonstrated that VLA models can leverage large-scale pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs) [20,1] as backbones to effectively generalize across various manipulation tasks. Despite their strong performance, most VLA models rely on large-scale architectures with billions of parameters, posing significant challenges for real-time deployment on resource-constrained robotic platforms. To address this limitation, lightweight VLA models such as SmolVLA [25] have been proposed, which employ a compact VLM backbone (e.g., SmolVLM2-500M) combined with a small action expert network for action generation via Flow Matching. This design significantly reduces computational cost while maintaining competitive performance. However, these lightweight VLA models suffer from a fundamental limitation: the action expert uses a single dense feed-forward network (FFN) to uniformly process all tasks. In multi-task robotic manipulation scenarios, different tasks (e.g., spatial reasoning, object manipulation, goal reaching) exhibit distinct action patterns that require different computational strategies. A single dense FFN is forced to encode all these diverse action generation patterns into a shared set of parameters, creating an inherent tension between model capacity and computational efficiency. Simply scaling up the action expert proportionally increases inference cost, negating the advantages of the lightweight design. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [11,24,13,6] offers an elegant solution to this dilemma. Unlike model compression techniques that trade represe