2.2 Mixture-of-Experts Models
Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [11] is a classical approach for scaling model capacity through conditional computation, maintaining multiple expert networks and using a gating mechanism to route each input to a sparse subset of experts. The sparsely-gated MoE layer [24] first demonstrated the scalability of this approach in deep learning. Subsequent works have advanced MoE along several directions, including simplified top-1 routing with load balancing [7] and largescale top-2 routing [16]. More recently, Mixtral [13] demonstrated that sparse MoE can match much larger dense models at a fraction of the inference cost. Two designs are particularly relevant to our work: DeepSeek-MoE [6] introduced shared experts that are always activated alongside routed experts, providing a stable baseline; AdaMoE [30] proposed adaptive scaling adapters that decouple expert selection from expert weighting. In the robotics domain, the application of MoE remains limited. Existing approaches to efficient robot learning primarily focus on model compression: knowledge distillation [8] transfers knowledge from large teacher models to smaller students, parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods like LoRA [10] reduce trainable parameters, and data augmentation techniques [29,21] improve sample efficiency. However, these methods fundamentally trade off representational capacity for computational savings. Sparse MoE offers a distinct advantage: it scales model capacity through multiple specialized experts while keeping the per-inference computation constant, making it ideally suited for multi-task robotic manipulation where different tasks demand different computational strategies but the deployment budget is fixed. Some works have explored MoE in multi-task reinforcement learning [27], but the integration of MoE into lightweight VLA models for action generation has not been systematically studied. To this end, our work introduces FFN-level MoE into the action expert of VLA models, drawing on the shared expert design of DeepSeek-MoE [6] and the adaptive routing of AdaMoE [30], combined with Sparse Upcycling [15] initialization that converts pre-trained dense checkpoints into sparse MoE models while preserving pre-trained knowledge.
