Flow Matching.SmolVLA uses Flow Matching [18,26] for action generation. Unlike diffusion-based approaches [9,5] that require many denoising steps, Flow Matching learns a continuous velocity field along a straight-line transport path from noise to the target action distribution. During training, given a ground-truth action sequencea∈R H×da (whereHis the action chunk size andd a is the action dimension) and sampled noiseε∼ N(0, I), a noisy action is constructed via linear interpolation at timestept∼Beta(1.5,1.0):
xt =t·ε+ (1−t)·a(2)
wheret∈[0,1]controls the interpolation between the clean actiona(att= 0) and pure noiseε(att= 1). The model learns to predict the velocity fieldv θ(xt, t) that transportsx t towarda, with the target velocityu t =ε−arepresenting the constant velocity along the straight-line path. The training loss minimizes the mean squared error between predicted and target velocities:
LF M =E t,ε ∥vθ(xt, t)−u t∥2 (3)
At inference, actions are generated by integrating the learned velocity field backward fromt= 1(pure noise) tot= 0(clean action) throughNsteps of Euler integration with step size∆t=−1/N.
