Impact of Load Balancing Loss.We examine the impact of the load balancing loss by comparing training with and without it. Removing the load balancing loss (λ= 0) causes the Goal success rate to drop from 93% to 90% (−3 points). Without this regularization, the router converges to a locally optimal allocation where some experts are underutilized (expert collapse), preventing the model from fully leveraging the MoE capacity. A weight ofλ= 0.01balances uniform utilization with routing flexibility.
5 Conclusion
In this paper, we propose SmolVLA-MoE, a framework that introduces FFNlevel sparse Mixture-of-Experts into the action expert of Vision-Language-Action models for multi-task robotic manipulation. By replacing dense FFN layers with MoE-FFN layers containing multiple parallel expert FFNs and a learnable tokenlevel router, SmolVLA-MoE enables each token to adaptively select specialized experts for action generation, scaling model capacity without increasing inference cost. Experiments on the LIBERO benchmark demonstrate that SmolVLA-MoE achieves superior performance over the SmolVLA baseline on all four task suites (Spatial, Object, Goal, and LIBERO-10), while maintaining the same total activated parameter count of∼500M as the dense baseline (with only∼100M activated in the action expert module). Systematic ablation studies reveal that top-1 routing outperforms top-2 routing on tasks requiring precise action generation, indicating that activating fewer but more specialized experts is preferable when expert differentiation is limited. The shared expert weight provides an effective control knob for balancing stability and specialization across different task types. Limitations and Future Work.Our evaluation is limited to the LIBERO benchmark in simulation. Future work includes extending to real-world tasks and other benchmarks (e.g., MetaWorld [28], RLBench [12]), exploring layerwise adaptive expert configurations, and combining MoE with complementary efficiency techniques such as quantization and knowledge distillation.
